Enhancement: Reduce memory usage when backing up very wide directories

Backing up a directory with a very large number of entries used about a kilobyte
of memory per entry, because every finished entry was held until the whole
directory had been walked and only then folded into the directory's tree. A
directory of a million files therefore needed roughly a gigabyte on top of the
tree itself, which was enough for a host with a memory cap to kill the backup.

Finished entries are now folded into the tree as the walk goes, so only a bounded
number are held at once; each entry's name is released once it has been used; and
the buffer holding the tree is sized from the directory's entry count instead of
being repeatedly grown and copied.

Backing up a single directory of 600000 entries under a 600 MiB memory cap
previously failed and now succeeds, and the same backup without a cap uses
39% less memory. The tree written is unchanged.

